A trough is $6$ m long. Its cross section is a right isosceles triangle with the right angle at the bottom and the top horizontal. Find the inflow needed to make the water depth rise at $0.05$ cm/min when the depth is $40$ cm. Give your answer in litres per minute; $1$ L $=1000$ cm³.

The diagram shows the triangular cross section of the water.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2,2)--(0,0)--(2,2)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(0,2);
\draw (-0.16,0.16)--(0,0.32)--(0.16,0.16);
\path (0,1) node[right] {$h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Use centimetres throughout, so the trough is $600$ cm long. The altitude bisects the right angle; each half is a $45^\circ$ right triangle, so the water-surface width is $2h$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2,2)--(0,0)--(2,2)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(0,2);
\draw (-0.16,0.16)--(0,0.32)--(0.16,0.16);
\path (0,1) node[right] {$h$};
\path (-1,2) node[above] {$h$};
\path (1,2) node[above] {$h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned} V&=\frac12(2h)h(600)\\ &=600h^2\\ \frac{dV}{dh}&=1200h\\ \frac{dV}{dt}&=\frac{dV}{dh}\times\frac{dh}{dt}\\ &=1200h(0.05) \end{aligned}$

At $h=40$:
$\begin{aligned} \frac{dV}{dt}&=1200(40)(0.05)\\ &=2400\ \text{cm}^3/\text{min}\\ &=2.4\ \text{L}/\text{min} \end{aligned}$

